   628→**Warning signs:**
   629→- Frame counts don't match expected program length
   630→- Register values jump unexpectedly mid-animation
   631→- Console errors about undefined frame indices
   632→
   633→### Pitfall 4: Variable Refresh Rate Desync
   634→
   635→**What goes wrong:** Animation plays at different speeds on 60Hz, 120Hz, and 144Hz monitors despite setting fixed FPS. Causes confusion in educational settings when instructor's demo runs faster than students' screens.
   636→
   637→**Why it happens:** requestAnimationFrame fires at monitor refresh rate. Without delta-time compensation, animation speed scales with refresh rate. 120Hz monitor runs animation 2x faster than 60Hz monitor.
   638→
   639→**How to avoid:**
   640→```javascript
   641→// Pattern already implemented in AnimationEngine.animate()
   642→animate = (currentTime = performance.now()) => {
   643→  if (!this.isPlaying) return;
   644→
   645→  const deltaTime = currentTime - this.lastFrameTime;
   646→
   647→  // Frame interval calculated from target FPS, not monitor refresh
   648→  const frameInterval = (1000 / this.fps) / this.speed;
   649→
   650→  // Only advance frame when enough time has passed
   651→  if (deltaTime >= frameInterval) {
   652→    this.stepForward();
   653→    // Accumulate leftover time to prevent drift
   654→    this.lastFrameTime = currentTime - (deltaTime % frameInterval);
   655→  }
   656→
   657→  this.rafId = requestAnimationFrame(this.animate);
   658→}
   659→```
   660→
   661→**Warning signs:**
   662→- Animation speed differs between computers
   663→- Frame timing feels inconsistent or stuttery
   664→- Speed multiplier doesn't produce expected changes
   665→
   666→### Pitfall 5: Forgetting Register $0 is Hardwired to Zero
   667→
   668→**What goes wrong:** Animation shows register $0 changing value during write-back stage, violating RISC architecture rules and confusing students learning pipeline concepts.
   669→
   670→**Why it happens:** Write-back logic doesn't check if target register is $0 (hardwired to zero in RISC architectures). Any instruction writing to $0 should succeed but have no effect.
   671→
   672→**How to avoid:**
   673→```javascript
   674→// In writeBack() function
   675→static writeBack(instruction, state) {
   676→  const newState = state.clone();
   677→  const writeReg = state.pipeline.MEM.writeReg;
   678→
   679→  // CRITICAL: Register 0 is hardwired to zero
   680→  if (writeReg !== 0) {
   681→    const oldVal = newState.registers[writeReg];
   682→    newState.registers[writeReg] = state.pipeline.MEM.writeData;
   683→
   684→    if (oldVal !== newState.registers[writeReg]) {
   685→      newState.changedRegisters.add(writeReg);
   686→    }
   687→  }
   688→  // If writeReg === 0, do nothing (write succeeds but has no effect)
   689→
   690→  return newState;
   691→}
   692→```
   693→
   694→**Warning signs:**
   695→- Register $0 highlights as "changed" during animation
   696→- Register $0 displays non-zero value after instruction
   697→- Students report confusion about "why $0 isn't zero"
